        "type": "prose",
        "content": "Virtual threads, introduced as part of Project Loom and standardized in Java 21, represent a paradigm shift in how we handle concurrent workloads on the Java Virtual Machine. Traditional platform threads map 1:1 to operating system threads, making them a scarce, expensive resource bound by kernel stack allocations (typically 1MB of memory) and expensive context switches. Virtual threads break this constraint by decoupling the application-level thread abstraction from the underlying OS thread, implementing an N:M scheduling model where millions of virtual threads (the N) are multiplexed across a much smaller pool of operating system threads known as carrier threads (the M). \n\nAt the core of this runtime architecture is the concept of mounting and unmounting. A virtual thread does not own a dedicated carrier thread for its entire lifecycle. Instead, when a virtual thread is runnable and scheduled to execute, it is 'mounted' onto a carrier thread—meaning its stack chunk is associated with the carrier thread's stack, and execution proceeds on the CPU. Crucially, when a virtual thread encounters a blocking operation (such as I/O reads, waiting on a lock via java.util.concurrent.locks, or invoking Thread.sleep), it is 'unmounted'. The JVM intercepts the blocking action, detaches the virtual thread's stack chunk from the carrier thread, and saves its state. The carrier thread is then immediately freed to mount and execute another runnable virtual thread. Once the blocking condition resolves, the virtual thread becomes eligible for scheduling again and is mounted onto an available carrier thread, potentially a completely different OS thread than the one it started on."
