    "title": "What is constructor overloading?",
    "category": "OOPs & Design Patterns",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "parsed_answer": [
      {
        "type": "prose",
        "content": "Constructor overloading is a compile-time polymorphism mechanism in Java that allows a class to have multiple constructors, each with a distinct parameter list. In the Java Virtual Machine, constructors are special methods (represented as <init> in bytecode) that initialize the state of an object after memory has been allocated on the heap. By providing multiple versions of these constructors, you enable flexible object instantiation, allowing consumers of your API to initialize objects with varying levels of detail—such as providing only mandatory fields or supplying a full configuration set. This pattern is essential for maintaining encapsulation while preventing the need for cumbersome 'setter-heavy' initialization or complex factory methods for simple use cases."
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "headers": [
          "Feature",
          "Default Constructor",
          "Overloaded Constructor"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Parameter List",
            "Empty",
            "Variable length/types"
          ],
          [
            "Purpose",
            "Basic object creation",
            "Flexible state initialization"
          ],
          [
            "Compiler Behavior",
            "Implicitly added if none defined",
            "Must be explicitly defined"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Constructor overloading relies on method signature uniqueness: the compiler distinguishes between constructors based on the number, type, and order of parameters, not the parameter names.",
          "The 'this()' keyword is the primary mechanism for constructor chaining, allowing one constructor to invoke another within the same class to reduce code duplication and ensure a single point of initialization logic.",
          "The 'this()' call must be the very first statement in the constructor body; failing to adhere to this results in a compile-time error because the object must be fully initialized before any secondary logic executes.",
          "Overloading does not affect the JVM's heap allocation phase; it merely dictates which sequence of bytecode instructions is executed to populate the object's instance fields."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "java",
        "content": "public class DatabaseConnection {\n    private String url;\n    private int timeout;\n\n    public DatabaseConnection(String url) {\n        this(url, 3000);\n    }\n\n    public DatabaseConnection(String url, int timeout) {\n        this.url = url;\n        this.timeout = timeout;\n    }\n}"
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "content": "A common production pitfall is 'telescoping constructor' syndrome, where you have too many overloaded constructors leading to unmaintainable code. If you find yourself needing more than three or four overloaded constructors, stop and refactor to the Builder Pattern. Also, remember that if you define any custom constructor, the compiler stops providing the default no-arg constructor, which can break frameworks like Spring or Hibernate that rely on reflection to instantiate beans via the no-arg constructor."
      }
    ]
