        "content": "A common point of confusion for developers transitioning to modern Java is the relationship between generic types like List<String> and List<Object>. Intuition from standard Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) suggests that since a String is a subclass of Object, a List of Strings should naturally be a subclass of a List of Objects. However, in the Java type system, List<String> is NOT a subclass of List<Object>. This restriction exists to preserve type safety and prevent runtime Heap Pollution via ClassCastException, which would break the core invariants of generics if covariance were allowed indiscriminately on mutable collections.\n\nTo safely achieve covariance—meaning the ability to read from a generic structure using a subtype—Java provides wildcard types with upper bounds, denoted by the extends keyword (e.g., List<? extends Object>). Conversely, contravariance handles write safety using lower bounds via the super keyword (e.g., List<? super String>). Understanding these distinctions is critical for writing robust generic APIs, leveraging the Get-Producer, Put-Consumer (PECS) principle coined by Joshua Bloch, and avoiding subtle compiler errors when working with collections."
