        "type": "prose",
        "content": "Designing a streaming service like Netflix requires solving the 'last mile' delivery problem at a massive scale. At the core, we rely on a Content Delivery Network (CDN) to push heavy binary blobs (video segments) to the network edge, minimizing latency by serving users from geographically proximate cache servers. Transcoding is the heavy-lift pre-processing phase where raw, high-bitrate mezzanine files are converted into multiple formats (H.264, HEVC, AV1) and resolutions (480p to 4K) to ensure compatibility across heterogeneous devices. Finally, Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming acts as the intelligent client-side controller. It continuously monitors the user's available bandwidth and CPU buffer state, dynamically switching between these pre-transcoded segments to prevent rebuffering, ensuring a smooth playback experience even on unstable mobile networks."
