    "title": "How do you test repository layer behavior?",
    "category": "Core Java & Fundamentals",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "parsed_answer": [
      {
        "type": "prose",
        "content": "Testing the repository layer is a critical bridge between your business logic and the persistence mechanism. When we talk about Repository Layer behavior, we are essentially validating the contract between your Java application and the database driver. The primary challenge here is avoiding the 'mocking trap'—where you mock the repository interface and end up testing your own mock configuration rather than the actual SQL execution or mapping logic. Exception handling is the most overlooked aspect; you must verify that your layer correctly translates vendor-specific database exceptions (like a SQLException or a Postgres-specific unique constraint violation) into your domain-specific DataAccessException hierarchy. Furthermore, when dealing with I/O and NIO, you must ensure that your repository correctly handles connection timeouts, socket resets, and stream interruptions without leaking resources or leaving the database connection pool in an inconsistent state."
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "headers": [
          "Testing Strategy",
          "Primary Benefit",
          "Trade-off"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "@DataJpaTest",
            "Full schema validation and Hibernate mapping check",
            "Requires a running database (H2/Testcontainers)"
          ],
          [
            "Mockito/Mocking",
            "Extremely fast execution",
            "Does not verify SQL syntax or constraint logic"
          ],
          [
            "Testcontainers",
            "Production-parity database environment",
            "High overhead on CI/CD build times"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Use @DataJpaTest in Spring Boot to slice the application context, which limits the beans loaded to only those related to persistence, significantly reducing startup time.",
          "Always test for 'Data Integrity Exceptions' by explicitly triggering constraint violations (e.g., inserting a duplicate primary key) to ensure your service layer receives the expected domain exception.",
          "Leverage Testcontainers to run a real database instance in a Docker container, ensuring that your native queries and dialect-specific syntax are validated against the actual database engine.",
          "Verify resource cleanup by checking that the repository layer does not leave open ResultSets or unclosed connections when an I/O exception occurs during stream processing."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "java",
        "content": "@DataJpaTest\n@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)\n@Testcontainers\nclass UserRepositoryTest {\n    @Container\n    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>(\"postgres:15\");\n\n    @Autowired\n    private UserRepository userRepository;\n\n    @Test\n    void shouldThrowExceptionOnDuplicateEmail() {\n        userRepository.save(new User(\"test@example.com\"));\n        assertThrows(DataIntegrityViolationException.class, () -> {\n            userRepository.save(new User(\"test@example.com\"));\n            userRepository.flush();\n        });\n    }\n}"
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "content": "A common production-grade mistake is forgetting to call flush() in your tests. Hibernate uses write-behind caching; if you don't force a flush, the SQL statement might not actually execute against the database during the test, meaning your exception handling logic is never triggered. Always verify the flush behavior when testing constraints."
      }
    ]
