        "content": "Trees represent hierarchical abstractions foundational to both low-level JVM memory management and high-performance in-memory indexing. When operating on tree data structures, mastering specific algorithmic patterns is non-negotiable for systems engineers. BST validations ensure that the binary search tree invariant holds globally, not just locally: every node in the left subtree must be strictly less than the current node, and every node in the right subtree strictly greater, necessitating explicit min/max boundary propagation during recursion. Traversals—specifically Inorder, Preorder, and Postorder—form the DFS family, where Inorder traversal of a valid BST naturally yields sorted output, Preorder facilitates serialization or cloning, and Postorder guarantees children are processed before parents, making it ideal for bottom-up metric aggregations like computing subtree sizes or heights. \n\nFinding the Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA) in a BST leverages the ordering property to achieve O(h) time complexity by comparing node values against the target values, whereas in a general binary tree, it requires exploring both subtrees and returning the node where targets diverge. Level Order Traversal implements Breadth-First Search (BFS) using a FIFO queue, visiting nodes level by level, which is critical for computing spatial properties or serializing tree layers. Finally, computing Max Depth and Diameter requires bottom-up recursive accumulation: max depth is simply the maximum height between left and right subtrees plus one, while the diameter—the length of the longest path between any two nodes—requires calculating the sum of left and right depths at every single node without assuming the path must pass through the root."
