        "content": "In early versions of Java, the String class was indeed implemented as a wrapper around a char array. However, this design was fundamentally flawed for modern memory management and internationalization. The primary reason String is not a simple char array today is the transition to compact strings and the need for immutability. By abstracting the internal representation away from a public char array, the JVM engineers gained the ability to optimize memory layout dynamically. Since Java 9, the 'Compact Strings' feature allows Strings to use byte arrays instead of char arrays if the content is Latin-1, effectively halving the memory footprint for a vast majority of web applications. If String were a raw char array, this optimization would be impossible because the API would be locked into a 2-byte-per-character fixed width."
